      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a body visualizer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A body visualizer (also spelled body visualiser) is an online tool that turns your height, weight and body measurements into a rotatable human figure, so you can see what those numbers look like. Some people call it a body simulator or a 3D body model tool. This one is free, runs in your browser, and also shows BMI, waist-to-hip ratio, waist-to-height ratio, an estimated body fat percentage and your body shape."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is this body visualizer free? Do I need to sign up?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is completely free, with no signup and nothing to install. Open the page, enter your numbers and use it. Nothing you enter is uploaded or stored."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does my body look like at a certain height and weight?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Enter your height and weight and the model draws a typical body for those numbers, with Auto-balance filling in the other measurements. For a closer match, add your bust or chest, waist and hips. To see two possibilities side by side, such as 150 lb and 165 lb at 5′6″, use Compare A/B or Overlay."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it a real 3D body visualizer? Can I rotate the model?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The figure is built from body cross-sections and rotates 360°. Use Front, ¾, Side and Back, drag the figure, press Spin, or use the arrow keys. It is an illustrative model rather than a scanned mesh, which keeps it fast, private and easy to compare."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use it as a female or male body visualizer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Choose Female, Male or Neutral under Model. The models differ in shoulders, neck, chest, waist, hips, glutes, legs, hands and feet, and the body shape names follow our female and male calculators. In Compare mode each body has its own model, so you can compare a woman and a man."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is this an AI body generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. It is a measurement-driven simulator: the same inputs always produce the same figure. It does not use AI to guess your body, does not ask for photos and never receives any."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I compare two bodies or do a height and weight comparison?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Choose Compare A/B. Use the Edit A and Edit B tabs to set each body’s model, height, weight and measurements. Both figures are drawn to the same scale and angle, and a table shows every change. Choose Overlay to lay B’s outline over A."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use it as a weight loss simulator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can preview a before and after: copy your current body to B, lower B’s weight and let Auto-balance rescale the measurements. Treat the result as a rough reference, not a target. The visualizer will not draw bodies below BMI 16, and the CDC describes steady loss of about 1 to 2 pounds per week as the pace most likely to be maintained. Ask a doctor or registered dietitian before setting a goal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which measurements do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only height and weight to start. For a better match, add bust or chest, waist and hips. Shoulder width, inseam, neck, upper arm and thigh are optional. The measuring table on this page shows where to place the tape."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is BMI and how is it shown here?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "BMI is weight divided by height squared (pounds ÷ inches² × 703, or kg ÷ m²). For adults 20 and older the CDC labels a BMI under 18.5 as underweight, 18.5 to 24.9 as healthy weight, 25 to 29.9 as overweight and 30 or higher as obesity. It is a screening measure, not a diagnosis, and it cannot tell muscle from fat."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How accurate is the body fat estimate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It uses the U.S. Navy circumference method, which needs height, neck and waist (plus hips for women). Individual results can be off by several percentage points, so use it to watch trends rather than as a medical measurement."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is my data saved or uploaded?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. All calculations and drawing happen in your browser. The Copy share link button puts your numbers in the link itself, after the # symbol, which browsers do not send to a server. Downloaded images are created on your device."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does the body visualizer work on iPhone and Android?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. On phones the model and the live analysis sit together in one pinned box at the top, so you can move the sliders below and watch the figure and numbers update. Drag the figure to rotate it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why doesn’t the figure look exactly like me?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The model is drawn only from the numbers you enter, so it cannot see muscle, bone structure, posture or where your body carries fat. Re-check your measurements, fill in the advanced fields, and remember that two people with the same measurements can look different."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between the body visualizer and the body type calculator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The body type calculator names your shape from bust or chest, waist and hip measurements. The visualizer works like a 3D body shape calculator: it draws a body from those numbers and more, and shows the same shape name live, so you can see your shape as well as read it."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
